# ThreatPulse

## About

ThreatPulse demonstrates my ability to turn raw threat intelligence into
prioritised security decisions. I designed it around the problem security leaders
actually face: not finding more alerts, but determining what deserves attention
first. Its collection, normalisation, enrichment and scoring pipeline shows
practical capability across threat intelligence, vulnerability management, data
engineering and risk-based prioritisation — and, importantly, an insistence that
evidence should drive the decision.

## So what?

Security teams are not short of alerts; they are short of a defensible answer to
one question: **what must we fix first, and why?**

A few seconds of the product working: **[ThreatPulse demo on YouTube](https://youtu.be/2Hq5_dG9_J8)**

[![ThreatPulse demo](https://img.youtube.com/vi/2Hq5_dG9_J8/hqdefault.jpg)](https://youtu.be/2Hq5_dG9_J8)

### What problem this solves, and for whom

Every catalogue a security team already has — the CISA KEV list, NVD, EPSS,
vendor advisories, CERT feeds — answers *what exists*. None of them answers *what
matters to us this week*. Ranking by CVSS alone over-weights theoretical bugs and
under-weights the ones that are actually being exploited, so the worklist ends up
sorted by a number that does not describe risk.

ThreatPulse turns those inputs into one ranked worklist: **one record per CVE**,
every contributing source attached to it, and a single transparent priority score
whose factors and weights are published with the record. The answer can be
reconstructed and defended, not just trusted.

- **Security leaders and vulnerability managers** get a defensible order of work:
  what to fix now, what to schedule, what to monitor — with the evidence behind
  each position.
- **SOC and threat-intelligence analysts** get triage that separates exploited,
  corroborated vulnerabilities from the ones that merely exist.
- **Engineers and architects** get a public, machine-readable, attributed API
  (schema 1.0) rather than a PDF or a dashboard they have to scrape.
- **Anyone auditing the reasoning** gets the model, the weights, the bands and
  the source register, so a score can be checked rather than believed.

It is not a scanner, not a SIEM and not an authority. It is the prioritisation
layer between the feeds a team already has and the decision it has to make.

## Methodology

The complete, current description also lives at
[threatpulse.iliadis.net/methodology](https://threatpulse.iliadis.net/methodology)
and in [backend/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md](backend/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md). This section
is the same method, in short.

### The question it answers

ThreatPulse exists to answer one question: **what deserves attention now — and
why?** Every published threat carries its score, the factors behind that score and
the evidence it rests on, so a decision can be reconstructed rather than taken on
trust. The dashboard displays the score the pipeline produced; it never
recalculates it and never re-ranks the list.

### The pipeline

`run_pipeline.py` is the only entry point. It runs seven stages in order and stops
at the first one that cannot succeed. Two properties hold throughout: the run is
**deterministic** (same inputs, same document) and **fail-closed** (anything
uncertain is refused, not guessed).

| Stage | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| **Collect** | 23 collector modules fetch each source over HTTPS from a separate server. Every collector reports `ok`, `degraded` or `failed`; a source that cannot be reached degrades to its last-known-good snapshot instead of destroying what the sources that did respond produced. |
| **Normalise** | 21 normalisers, one per source family, turn payloads into typed observations (`source`, record id, timestamps, entities, TLP label). Feed content is untrusted, so every field is validated, bounded and sanitised; a record that cannot be trusted is rejected with a reason rather than coerced. |
| **Correlate** | Observations about the same subject are merged into one intelligence object with all of their evidence attached. Only the highest-priority entity kind an observation carries is used, so one advisory naming two CVEs is not counted twice for the same reason. |
| **Enrich** | MITRE ATT&CK, CAPEC and Sigma add technique, actor, CWE-route and detection-coverage context. Enrichment is context, never proof of activity: it cannot set exploitation status and cannot move a score on its own. |
| **Score** | `threatpulse_priority_v0.3` scores every vulnerability threat from the evidence present (below). Every factor is optional and the weights actually used are published with the record. |
| **Quality gate** | The exact bytes that would be sent are checked: schema 1.0 structure, provenance on every record, TLP markings, volume against a configured minimum and against the previous run, and request size. A refusal is a run failure, not a warning. |
| **Publish** | The document is split so every request stays under 7 MiB, below a common 8 MB `post_max_size`. Every chunk carries the same publication id, so the API assembles one publication and upserts rather than duplicating. |

### Identity and correlation

A published threat's identifier is derived from **the CVE alone**:
`sha256("threatpulse:cisa_kev:" + CVE)`, rendered as a UUID. The namespace is a
compatibility constant kept from the KEV-only era, not the current source key, for
three reasons that matter operationally:

- a CVE first published from enrichment evidence keeps the same identifier when it
  later enters the KEV catalogue, instead of creating a second record;
- the same CVE reported by an advisory, a vendor PSIRT and the KEV list converges
  on one object with three pieces of evidence, not three objects;
- identity is deterministic, so re-running the pipeline cannot duplicate a threat
  and a republication is an update rather than an insertion.

Malware-family records and indicator clusters were retired on 2 November 2026, so
every published threat today is keyed on a CVE. Groups keyed on anything else (an
actor, a country) are counted and reported, not published.

### The prioritisation model

One model scores every vulnerability, whether or not it appears in the CISA KEV
catalogue. It reads only evidence that actually exists: exploitation assessments,
EPSS percentile, CVSS or a stated severity, independent corroboration, deadline
pressure or an available fix, the source's own ransomware and forensic
determinations, and how recent the signal is.

| Factor | Weight | What it captures |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `exploitation` | 28% | Whether the threat is observed or assessed to be exploited. |
| `epss` | 20% | Where the CVE sits in the EPSS distribution of exploitation probability. |
| `impact` | 18% | Severity of the technical consequence. |
| `evidence` | 10% | How many independent sources describe the same vulnerability. |
| `urgency` | 8% | Remediation deadline pressure, such as a CISA due date. |
| `ransomware` | 6% | Known or suspected ransomware campaign use. |
| `recency` | 5% | How recently the signal appeared or was refreshed. |
| `first_seen` | 3% | When ThreatPulse first collected it — not when it was published. |
| `forensic` | 2% | Whether forensic triage is indicated. |

**Every factor is optional.** A factor the evidence does not support contributes
nothing and is not reported: `score_factors` names exactly what produced the
number and `score_weights` holds only those weights. The score therefore *falls*
when evidence is missing rather than being redistributed upwards, so a thin record
scores low — which is the right answer for priority.

Two rules keep the number honest:

- **A refusal beats a guess.** Fewer than two independent sources raises
  `InsufficientEvidence` — unless one of them is the CISA KEV catalogue, which is a
  corroborated determination in its own right. So does the absence of any
  exploitation, EPSS or impact signal. A record the model cannot support is not
  published at all.
- **Exploitation is never low.** A record whose exploitation factor is at or above
  90 is floored at 50, so it cannot rank below an unremarkable record that merely
  happens to carry a CVSS.

The previous model (`threatpulse_priority_v0.1`, six factors, KEV-derived) still
runs for KEV-shaped records and is published beside the current score as
`metadata.legacy_score`. That is the audit trail for the unification: the change can
be checked record by record and reversed without recomputing anything.

### Bands

The band is a label for the score, not a separate judgement. The published
`severity` of a record *is* this band.

| Score | Band | Guidance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 90–100 | Critical | Top of the model — immediate attention. |
| 75–89.99 | High | Strong signals — schedule action now. |
| 50–74.99 | Medium | Relevant, but not yet urgent. |
| 25–49.99 | Low | Monitor. |
| 0–24.99 | Informational | Context only. |

On the 2 November 2026 publication (3,558 records) that produced 3.5% critical,
27.5% high, 24% medium, 10% low and 35% informational. A narrow critical band is
the point — a label covering a quarter of the catalogue is not a prioritisation
anyone can act on — and the large informational group is the honest cost of
publishing thin records rather than withholding them. The thresholds are
**provisional**: they were recalibrated when the models were unified, because the
old 85/70 split labelled 23% of a publication "critical".



### Provenance and the handling rule

- **Every source is declared before it may be read.** The register in
  `backend/data/attribution.json` records each source's provider, licence,
  attribution text, TLP default and current contribution. An undeclared source is
  refused outright — an absence of paperwork is not permission — and the register is
  published at `/api/cti/v1/attribution.php` and on the Sources page, including the
  sources deliberately *not* ingested.
- **Attribution is provenance, not endorsement.** Listing a source states where a
  signal came from, not that the source endorses ThreatPulse or the reverse.
- **Each threat carries its evidence.** Source key, source name, source record
  identifier and retrieval timestamp are stored per record. A record that rests on
  several sources says so, rather than merging them into one unattributed claim.
- **Only TLP:CLEAR/WHITE is ingested.** ThreatPulse publishes to an open,
  unauthenticated dashboard, so it may only republish material released for
  unrestricted public use. TLP:GREEN, AMBER, AMBER+STRICT and RED are never ingested,
  stored or republished. An item marked more restrictively than its source default is
  rejected with a reason, so one over-marked advisory cannot discard the rest of a
  feed; an unrecognised marking is refused rather than assumed public.
- **A failed source is reported, not hidden.** The run records `ok`, `degraded` or
  `failed` per source, with the observation count that justifies the verdict and the
  failure reason. That report travels with the publication (`source_health.php`),
  because "why is today's count low?" is a question the reader is entitled to ask.

### The gate, and what reaches the public

The quality gate validates the exact bytes that would be transmitted and refuses
the run rather than the record: schema structure, provenance on every record, TLP
markings, volume against the configured minimum and against the previous
publication, and request size. A refused publication is never sent, and a failed
run is visible in the service journal.

What is published is a complete snapshot — `data/latest_publication.json`, schema
1.0, replaced wholesale — served by a read-only API: threats, threat by id or CVE,
vulnerabilities, sources, the attribution register, publications, the field-level
delta against the previous publication, source health and status, plus a
machine-readable discovery document. Every response carries provenance; timestamps
come from the pipeline and are interpreted and displayed as UTC, so a viewer's time
zone cannot shift a date silently; API responses are cached for 60 seconds and the
dashboard reuses that window rather than forcing repeated requests.

### What the interface does not do

The frontend is a presentation layer, not a second opinion. It does not recalculate
the score, re-rank the list, infer a severity from a CVSS, or fill in a missing
factor. Values that are absent are shown as absent, and a record with no factor
breakdown says so instead of rendering an empty grid. That is deliberate: if the
interface invented a number, the published evidence would no longer explain the
decision.

### Limits

- The weights and thresholds are a **documented judgement**, not a validated ground
  truth. They are published so they can be argued with, and the bands are marked
  provisional.
- The score is a **general-purpose priority**, not an organisational risk score: it
  has no asset inventory, business context or exposure data behind it. It tells you
  what to look at first, not what your organisation would lose.
- **Coverage is bounded by the register.** The Sources page is the authoritative
  list of what is and is not ingested; source health explains a thin day.
- Enrichment (ATT&CK, CAPEC, Sigma) is context, never evidence of activity.
- Third-party data can be wrong, incomplete or stale. ThreatPulse supports, and does
  not replace, professional judgement; findings should be validated against the
  organisation's own vulnerability-management, threat-intelligence and risk
  processes before action.

---

![ThreatPulse — actionable threat intelligence for a more resilient enterprise](./threatpulse-banner.png)

Prioritise the vulnerabilities and threats that deserve attention first, using
normalised, scored threat intelligence.

ThreatPulse is a threat-intelligence and vulnerability-prioritization application
that collects and normalizes current cyber-threat information to help cybersecurity
professionals identify which vulnerabilities and threats deserve attention first. It
brings together sources such as the CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV)
catalogue and applies prioritization and scoring so that security teams can focus on
the items most likely to matter rather than working through unfiltered feeds.
ThreatPulse is an independently developed analytical application intended to
support, not replace, professional judgement: its prioritization reflects the
sources and scoring applied at the time of viewing and is not an assessment,
certification or endorsement by CISA or any other authority. Users should validate
findings against their own vulnerability-management, threat-intelligence and risk
processes before acting on them.

Live at [threatpulse.iliadis.net](https://threatpulse.iliadis.net/). API discovery
document: [api/index.html](api/index.html).

## What is in this repository

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `frontend/` | React 19 + TypeScript + Vite dashboard. Static build, deployed next to the API, no server runtime. |
| `api/` | PHP read API (schema 1.0) plus the restricted write endpoint, and the human-readable API documentation at `api/index.html`. |
| `backend/` | Python pipeline: collect, normalise, correlate, enrich, score, quality gate, publish. Standard library only. |
| `deployment/` | Deployment checklist, origin restriction, the build-time stamping script and systemd/MySQL assets. |
| `api-tests/` | Contract suite that exercises the API against a local PHP + SQLite harness. |
| `social-preview.jpg` | 1280 × 640 image to upload as the repository's GitHub social preview (Settings → Social preview); the same artwork is the site's `og:image` and the dashboard hero. |

## Quick start

```bash
# dashboard
cd frontend
npm ci
npm run dev            # http://localhost:5173, /api proxied to the live site
npm test               # unit tests
npm run build          # type-check, bundle, stamp the origin secret

# pipeline
cd backend
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .   # 629 tests
python3 run_pipeline.py --dry-run            # collect and score, transmit nothing

# API contract
cd frontend
npm run verify:api     # local PHP + SQLite harness
```

## Deployment

See [deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md](deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md). The site is reachable only
through Cloudflare: both `.htaccess` files require a header the edge injects, and the
value is stamped at build time from a file that is not in git. The reasoning, the
rotation procedure and the verification matrix are in
[deployment/ORIGIN_RESTRICTION.md](deployment/ORIGIN_RESTRICTION.md).

## Data and attribution

Every ingested source is recorded with its provider, licence and attribution text in
`backend/data/attribution.json`. That register is published by the API at
`/api/cti/v1/attribution.php` and shown on the site Sources page.

The register records summaries, not legal advice. Each source remains subject to its
own current terms; verify them before relying on a source commercially.

## Security

Please report vulnerabilities privately, not in a public issue. The policy, the
response targets, the safe-harbour terms and the scope are in
[SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md); the machine-readable contact defined by RFC 9116 is
served at
[`/.well-known/security.txt`](https://threatpulse.iliadis.net/.well-known/security.txt).

